very pleased to see the former Justice  Minister and of course Rishi sunak  supporter Victoria Atkins joins us on  the program Ms Jackson it's lovely to  see you and first things first can we  get a guarantee that we're not all going  to be back here in a few months time  electing another conservative leader and  prime minister  uh yes because uh  Rishi got  get together as members of parliament to  debate the great issues of the day the  reception which she got from across the  party was so uh inspiring to see and  also so comforting when you look at the  range of supporters across the party I  think we have a real chance here of you  lighting and the reason we want to unite  is because we realize the very very  grave issues facing the country indeed  and all of us all of us whatever side of  the party we come from all of us want to  unite to work together to deliver for  our country and our constituents with  respect that's exactly what those trust  supporters told me seven weeks ago today  they went too far too far too fast with  some of the measures that were announced  I wanted desperately wanted Liz to  succeed I'm a conservative I also want  to deliver for my constituents but I  think now that we've seen the reaction  of the markets and also there has been a  much greater debate as to the  precariousness of the world economy as  well as our own domestic economy economy  I I think and I'm convinced given the  reaction of members of parliament  yesterday and the unity that people  showed in uniting behind Rishi that we  will be able to move forward yeah we're  elected by our construction to deliver  we want to do that  I mean presumably you're not talking  about people like Nadine Doris who says  a general election is now necessary  presumably you're not talking about  another end piece of Christopher chope  suggesting that uh that British sunak  supporters are hyenas uh certainly not  Mr Campbell Bannerman who Twisted a  picture last night of a snake on the  doorstep of number 10 with Rishi sunak's  face this doesn't strike me as a party  in the throes of unity  now I mean the last chap you mentioned  just then isn't a member of parliament  but um  yeah there are going to be uh look we  understand this is a it's been a very  very tough uh six weeks for us as a  party of course the country would be  saying well hang on a minute it's been a  very tough six weeks for us as well  which is why I think of course there  will be some bruised feelings among some  people but actually what we saw  yesterday was the overwhelming majority  of people coming together understanding  that we now have to focus on tackling  inflation we have to stabilize the  markets we have to try to help the bank  of England with the the difficult  decisions they have coming ahead of them  with interest rates and so on we have to  do everything we can to stabilize the  economy because if we can do that then  we can start to deliver the vision that  Rishi set out over the summer and that  we will all look forward to supporting  when there is a general election  sure there will however be people  watching this morning who do not simply  believe what you see because of the  destabilizing effect that the  conservatives have laterally had on on  the economy so so just in terms of  policy what is Rishi sunat going to do  to deal with that 40 odd billion pound  black hole as identified by pretty much  every Financial commentator I can find  well the first I'm very very conscious  that we need to regain people's trust  and so uh Rishi being the former  Chancellor that he is don't forget he uh  set up the furlough scheme had to deal  with the nightmare of the coveted  pandemic within a month of becoming  Chancellor he managed it superbly and  said we knew all of that what is he  going to do about the black hole so in  terms of the black hole he will be  getting to work immediately with the  chancellor to address these issues  you'll you'll appreciate I'm not in the  room with them in those discussions  indeed but my goodness me we have a we  have someone in office who doesn't need  um lessons in how the you know how the  treasury works or whatever he will  absolutely get on with the job as soon  as he walks through that great big black  door but are there areas that should be  protected the health service for example  the three percent on on defense spending  well we um again Rishi has said that we  stood and and won an election in 2019 on  that Manifesto and he wants to stick to  that Manifesto and that seems to me uh  absolutely the right way forward and so  we're committed completely committed to  the NHS we know that this winter is  going to be a very difficult one for a  whole host of reasons not least the  backlogs that accumulated during the as  a result of the pandemic but also with  defense he's been very very clear that  for example we will continue to do  everything we need to do not just to  defend our country but also to help  Ukraine in particular which of course  that war that Dreadful war in Ukraine is  some of the reason for some of the  economic turmoil that the whole world is  seeing as a result of energy prices  escalating  but but we are going to be entering into  aren't we a period of austerity and it  will be a period of austerity in which  we have a prime minister who via  marriage is a billionaire so when Rishi  sunak stands up and says that there  isn't going to be any money to give  nurses to put their salaries up that  there isn't going to be money to pay for  school repairs or perhaps even some  covert compliance ventilation going on  in there it will be a billionaire saying  so how do you think the public will  react to that  so if I can just stack a little bit from  that because um as I say it wouldn't be  right for me to appear even by inference  to confirm  um what you're saying in that question I  think we've got to give Rishi and the  chancellor a bit of time and space to  work out the plans where you know Rishi  very much would once the a statement to  go ahead on 31st of October but  decisions will be made on that we've got  to take this step by step in terms of  how Rishi uh speaks the public  communicates with the public relates to  the public I think we saw it again in  the pandemic you know I'm not asking you  to imagine that he's good at this we  know he's good at this because we've  seen it as we were all huddled around  our televisions during lockdown and  we're reassured by Rishi saying this is  what I'm going to do for you we're going  to put our arms around you and so I'm  very very confident that we will see  more of Rishi in that mode if you like  reassuring us stabilizing the markets  and it is notable by the way the  reaction of the markets over the last 24  hours I think that again gives us some  small grounds for optimism and so I  think stabilizing the markets tackling  inflation because as Rishi said  throughout the summer that is the killer  with a lot of this you know if we've got  food inflation if we've got wage  inflation these are all difficult  difficult issues and Rishi has the  experience and the expertise to deal  with this  all of which makes whoever he puts  around the cabinet table with them  incredibly incredibly important  particularly of course the job in number  11. he's talked about building a  government of all the talents what do  you understand him to mean by that I  mean are we basically not going to about  to see a member of the ERG sitting  around the cabinet table  I think we have an enormous range of  talents across the party I'm not going  to name names if you'll forgive me  because I I don't want to make enemies  at seven o'clock in the morning but uh  fair enough now yeah but we have a huge  range of talent across the party and I  think one of the um sadnesses of uh  perhaps the last six weeks was that not  all of that Talent was really used and  really exploited if you like um by the  previous administration so I'm really  looking forward to some excellent  colleagues and we've got we've got a  huge range of experience in teaching  police and and so on across the board  I'd love to see some of our expertise  and experience come into our government  so that we're shaping policies and  delivering policies that really make a  difference for our country  meet the case then if you can for the  country not being given a vote on what  is you know the the third prime minister  we've had in you know a handful of  months why is no not the right time I  mean you've gone through the decision  breaking process now twice internally  within the conservative party by your  own admission you got it wrong the first  time you know you're keeping your  fingers crossed on this one I mean why  not a little more than that you know  forgive me a little bit more than that  I'm very confident about uh this  Administration uh in terms of demand I  think there are two actually two primary  reasons first of all it's completely  practical which is my goodness me we  have got a lot to deal with at the  moment we have to stabilize the markets  we have to stabilize the economy we have  to ensure for example that the energy  price guarantee that Liz introduced  that's a phenomenal package that would  make sense that would make sense that  would make sense if the first thing I  said to his MPS was not unite or die I  mean this is all about the existential  crisis the conservative party is facing  it's nothing to do with country  no no and the second reason if I may for  um the the question about an election  and forgive me this is a bit of sort of  constitutional uh law in the in the  middle of the beginning of the morning  for people who've just woken up but uh  we do we elect we let elect a party you  know it's a conservative Manifesto in  2019. uh we don't have a presidential  system and I am responsible and uh I am  representative of my constituents but  I'm also accountable to them so we stood  on that Manifesto we deliver it and of  course in Parliament it is the let's  trust did not deliver the 2019 Manifesto  who can command a majority and and His  Majesty the king will invite uh that  person to deliver a government look I  think I mean again I think Liz would  accept that she stepped away from the  2019 Manifesto there were particular  issues that's my point why should we no  believe you that Rishi sunai will not I  mean the last prime minister that was in  post for seven weeks said exactly the  same thing you know Cameron give us my  plan put it to the country but so what's  the difference I mean you've already  shown that the conservative party cannot  be trusted to the 2019 Manifesto  commitment with the last well current  prime minister because because Rishi  said we will stick to the 2019 Manifesto  it's as simple as that so yeah we will  continue to see the focus on leveling up  we'll continue to see the focus on the  NHS on on security my own personal area  of sort of expertise and interest if you  will is supporting violence or attacking  violence against women girls supporting  victims of domestic abuse and so on we  want to continue all of these pieces of  work as we deal with this very very  difficult issue of our domestic economy  in the context of the international  problems that other countries are facing  as well so look I appreciate that it's  been a pretty you know it's been a  brutal six weeks in politics for lots of  people it's been a very very fast pace  but we now behind Rishi will have a  stability and confidence and reassurance  that I hope will begin to make a real  difference to people's lives over the  coming weeks and months  I I know I've got to let you go but I  can't let let you go without this cheeky  little question at the end have you got  a job  [Laughter]  yeah indeed okay Victoria Atkins lovely  to have you on the program Take Care  thank you so much take care

